WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:03.180
YUFENG GUO: Not everyone
uses SQL, but if you do,

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:05.610
wouldn't it be nice to be
able to use machine learning

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:07.690
within your SQL environment?

00:00:07.690 --> 00:00:10.080
BigQuery ML does just that.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:11.400
Stay tuned to find out how.

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:23.400
Welcome to AI Adventures, where
we explore the art, science,

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.380
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:27.810
My name is Yufeng Guo,
and on this episode,

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:30.470
we're going to try
out BigQuery ML, which

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:32.240
enables you to
create and execute

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:34.490
machine learning
models in BigQuery

00:00:34.490 --> 00:00:37.130
using standard SQL queries.

00:00:37.130 --> 00:00:40.670
Currently, BigQuery
ML, or BQML for short,

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:44.150
supports linear regression,
binary logistic regression,

00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:47.360
and multiclass
logistic regression.

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:49.460
Linear regressions
are models which

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:53.540
predict a numerical value, like
an insurance premium or a home

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:54.910
value.

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:57.240
Binary logistic
regressions are models

00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:59.660
which predict one
of two classes,

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:01.790
like identifying
whether a machine needs

00:01:01.790 --> 00:01:05.060
to be checked for maintenance
work, a yes or a no, or perhaps

00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:08.840
more famously, whether an
image is a cat or a dog.

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:11.720
Multiclass logistic
regressions are models which

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:14.400
predict more than two classes--

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:19.040
for example, whether or not
you should buy, hold, or sell

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:22.100
a stock, or if, say,
traffic on the highway

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:25.100
is light, medium,
heavy, or a parking lot.

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:28.070
Let's see what BQML
looks like in practice.

00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:30.530
You can access it
via the BigQuery UI

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:33.350
or through the
command line and API.

00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:36.020
Today, we'll look at
it on the BigQuery UI,

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:39.500
but the queries we'll use
are identical to if you use

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:41.690
the command line or the API.

00:01:41.690 --> 00:01:44.990
I've got some sample data here
from Google Analytics, taken

00:01:44.990 --> 00:01:47.420
from a BigQuery public data set.

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:49.760
We did an episode on the
BigQuery public data sets

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:52.400
program a while back, so
be sure to check that out

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:54.920
to learn more about all
the public data sets that

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:57.270
are free for you to use.

00:01:57.270 --> 00:01:59.450
Now, let's take a look
at our data set today.

00:01:59.450 --> 00:02:02.330
Notice that we're looking
specifically at whether or not

00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:04.190
a transaction took place.

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:07.590
That's what we'll be
trying to predict later on.

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:10.250
Now, what will we base
these predictions on?

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:13.080
We have the operating
system of the user's device,

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:16.790
whether it was a mobile device
or not, the country of origin,

00:02:16.790 --> 00:02:20.300
and the number of page views
that the user performed.

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:21.840
I have a feeling
that last column,

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:23.930
that's probably going
to be pretty useful.

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:27.880
Notice also that we're pulling
data from this sample data

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:33.320
set from between August
2016 through June of 2017.

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:35.540
OK, let's try building
a model to see

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:38.660
if we can make some predictions
based on these criteria,

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:40.130
on these columns.

00:02:40.130 --> 00:02:41.600
We'll add a create
model statement

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:45.360
at the top with the table name,
and give our sample a name--

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:46.978
say, just sample model.

00:02:46.978 --> 00:02:49.520
And we'll pass in some options,
including the fact that we're

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:51.202
doing a logistic regression.

00:02:51.202 --> 00:02:52.910
So we're just going
to say yes or no, one

00:02:52.910 --> 00:02:55.670
of two options, whether or
not a transaction took place.

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:58.107
I'll just put that at the
top of our SELECT statement.

00:02:58.107 --> 00:02:59.690
And that's really
the only edit you'll

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:03.475
need to turn your
query into a model.

00:03:03.475 --> 00:03:04.850
Now, while that's
running, I want

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:07.520
to point out that the
aliasing on that first column

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:10.760
of transactions, to
call it a label, that

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:12.080
wasn't a coincidence.

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:16.730
BQML looks specifically
for a column named label

00:03:16.730 --> 00:03:19.520
so that it can figure out which
column you want to predict on

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:21.750
and which are the columns
you want as inputs.

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:24.770
So if you don't want to
create a column called label,

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:28.040
for some reason, or if you have
a column actually called label

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:29.810
that you don't want
to use this label,

00:03:29.810 --> 00:03:33.140
you can set the input
label calls option

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:35.420
in the create model statement.

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:39.200
Say we call that column
transactions instead, right,

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:41.030
and there was no
column called labels.

00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:43.010
Then we could do
something like this,

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:45.050
where we pass in the
name transactions

00:03:45.050 --> 00:03:49.670
as the column to look
for as our label.

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:52.130
What's really
handy about BQML is

00:03:52.130 --> 00:03:55.550
that you don't need to move your
data anywhere to take advantage

00:03:55.550 --> 00:03:58.820
of its features, since it's
built right into BigQuery.

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:02.090
This means that it can
serve as a really easy way

00:04:02.090 --> 00:04:05.690
to get a first look at any
notable patterns in your data.

00:04:05.690 --> 00:04:07.370
Once your model has
started training,

00:04:07.370 --> 00:04:10.050
you can check out the training
statistics if you want--

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:11.850
click on the model in
the left-hand panel

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:15.770
and go over to the Model
Stats tab down below.

00:04:15.770 --> 00:04:17.720
There, you can see
the train progress

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:20.450
as it works through the data
set and improves the model

00:04:20.450 --> 00:04:23.150
performance with each pass.

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:26.450
Now, when the create model
statement finishes running,

00:04:26.450 --> 00:04:30.800
it's time to run evaluation
using ML.EVALUATE.

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:33.810
We'll take the original SQL
query that we had for the data

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:37.100
set and wrap it in
ML.EVALUATE, passing

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:40.280
in the newly created
model and then selecting

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:41.820
from that result.

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:44.120
You will get an
output that includes

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:46.723
a number of different
metrics of your model.

00:04:46.723 --> 00:04:48.140
Notice that we've
actually queried

00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:51.410
dates that are outside of the
range of the training data.

00:04:51.410 --> 00:04:54.380
And specifically, it's the month
immediately following the dates

00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:56.230
that we use to train on.

00:04:56.230 --> 00:04:57.980
We don't want to pass
in the training data

00:04:57.980 --> 00:04:59.397
for evaluation,
because that would

00:04:59.397 --> 00:05:00.990
be like cheating on a test.

00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:03.890
Finally, it's time
to run Predict.

00:05:03.890 --> 00:05:07.250
Here, we'll wrap that same
query we used during evaluation

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:12.180
with the ML.PREDICT function,
and pass the model in as well.

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:13.700
This particular
prediction groups

00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:16.250
up the countries so we can
see what their predictions are

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:18.640
for each country ranked.

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:21.340
We can also see that for
this particular data set,

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:24.400
most of the transactions
came from the United States,

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:26.200
though it might be
worth diving in deeper

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:28.900
to see how these match
up across mobile devices

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:30.920
and different operating systems.

00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:34.150
So that's BigQuery ML in
a nutshell-- a simple way

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:36.400
to do some easy
machine learning right

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:38.380
within your SQL environment.

00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:41.110
I'm looking forward to
seeing what models you build.

00:05:41.110 --> 00:05:44.360
Thanks for watching this
episode of Cloud AI Adventures.

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:47.110
And if you enjoyed it, be
sure to hit that Like button

00:05:47.110 --> 00:05:49.660
and subscribe to get all
the latest updates right

00:05:49.660 --> 00:05:50.920
when they come out.

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:53.950
For now, check out BigQuery
ML and start building

00:05:53.950 --> 00:05:56.450
your first model today.

